TAIL(1)                       User Commands

NAME
    tail - print the end of a file and follow additions

SYNOPSIS
    tail [-f] [-n count | -c count] [file...]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the last ten lines by default. -n selects lines, -c selects bytes.
    Counts must be nonnegative. Long lines are retained intact. -f polls regular
    files for appended bytes every 200 ms and resumes at the start after
    truncation. It follows the open file, not a replacement at the same path.
    Ctrl-C stops following. With no files or -, reads stdin.

SEE ALSO
    sh(1), man(1), index(7)
